<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="chlorophyll a degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5098" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Chlorophyll degradation, corresponding to the loss of green color, is an integral part of leaf senescence and fruit ripening |CITS:[16113212],[15012204],[10767575],[12675736][ 16669755]|. Chlorophyll degradation in land plants may follow one of two routes, likely depending on the physiogical context in which it occurs. This pathway, |FRAME: PWY-5098| may be cytosolic and may operate during fruit senescence and in response to pathogens and wounding |CITS: [15598807][18633118]|. Meanwhile, |FRAME: PWY-6927|, begins in the chloroplast and may be the primary route for chlorophyll degradation in senescing leaves in |FRAME: ORG-5993| and other species, such as broccoli (|FRAME: TAX-3712|) |CITS: [19304936][20727617]|. Evidence for the existence of that alternative pathway was collected after it was observed that two Arabidopsis chlorophyllases (CLH1 and CLH2) are not required for chlorophyll breakdown during leaf senescence |CITS: [17996203]|. Both of these pathways appear to lead to the production of a colourless, blue-fluorescing intermediate (|FRAME: CPD-7064| or pFCC) &lt;i&gt;via&lt;/i&gt; the formation of a red chlorophyll catabolite (RCC). The final steps of chlorophyll catabolism are species-specific and involve a diversity of modifications of pFCC before the catabolites are finally stored in the vacuole in the form of |FRAME:NCCS|, or NCCs |CITS:[15012204],[16448336]|. NCCs share a common tetrapyrrolic skeleton with an oxygenolytically opened porphyrin macrocycle |CITS:[KRAEUTER03]|, with peripheral, species-specific modification at several side chains within different NCCs. The final conversion of modified FCCs to NCCs is a non-enzymatic reaction catalyzed by the acidic vacuolar pH. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; One of the first step in this route of chlorophyll degradation is dephytylation, or removal of the phytol moiety of the compound. A substantial portion of this phytol remains esterified with fatty acids |CITS:[PATTERSON93]| and with acetic acid |CITS:[BORTLIK90]|, whilst the rest is photooxidatively converted into various isoprenoid compounds |CITS:[RONTANI96]|. This step is catalyzed by chlorophyllases which have been described in several plants species (for review see |CITS:[16448336]|) and acts primarily on chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;. The second enzyme of the pathway, Mg dechelatase, remains elusive. This enzyme catalyzes the release of Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; from chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; in exchange from two protons to form pheophorbide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;. Since the purification of the protein has been unsuccessful and has led to the detection of a low-molecular-weight compound rather than a protein, the removal of Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; from chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; has been postulated to be performed by a &apos;Mg-dechelating substance&apos;, or MCS. It is uncertain whether MCS represents an active cofactor of Mg-dechelatase. The structure of either Mg-dechelatase or MCS remains to be determined |CITS:[16448336]|. The next two steps are decisive in the loss of green colour. The ring-opening of, and production of the first identifiable colourless product pFCC from pheophorbide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; requires two proteins from gerontoplasts membranes and stroma. Opening of the ring through oxygenolysis of pheophorbide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; is catalyzed by the membrane-localized, nonheme iron-containing monooxygenase, pheophorbide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; oxygenase (PaO) which yields red bilin RCC. This compound is channeled to RCCR which further reduces it to form pFCC. Both enzymes use reduced ferredoxin as immediate electron donor |CITS:[15012204]|.</Description>
