    <DataNode elementId="AT4G37000-MONOMER" textLabel="AT4G37000-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q8LDU4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana RCCR&lt;/i&gt; gene was expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; and shown to encode a protein with red chlorophyll catabolite (RCC) reductase activity. This enzyme catalyzes the reduction of the C20/21 double bond of RCC |CITS:[10743659]| to form a primary fluorescent catabolite (pFCC). This step depends on the presence of reduced ferredoxin (Fd) which is generated either by NADPH through the pentose phosphate cycle or by the action of photosystem I |CITS:[12223835]|. The enzyme was shown to catalyze the formation of two stereoisomeric forms of pFCC: pFCC-1 and pFCC-2 (as well as another unidentified third catabolite) |CITS:[10743659]|. Interestingly, whilst the native enzyme would catabolize the formation of both stereoisomers, the &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; recombinant protein would predominantly produce pFCC-1. It has been postulated that RCCR stereospecificity might be defined through its interaction with |FRAME:AT3G44880-MONOMER| |CITS:[12223836],[10743659]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G37000-MONOMER" />
