    <DataNode elementId="AT1G19670-MONOMER" textLabel="Chlorophyllase-1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT1G19670.1" dataSource="TAIR gene name" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="495" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana CLH1&lt;/i&gt; gene was expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; and shown to encode a protein with chlorophyllase activity. This enzyme catalyzes the hydrolysis of the ester bond of both chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; (with a preference for chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;), producing chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;, respectively, and phytol |CITS:[11950974]|. The hydrolytic esterase activity of the enzyme appears to be very specific to chlorophylls. Although chlorophyllases have been shown in several species to be glycosylated &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[terpstra81],[tsuchiya97]|, and their activity enhanced by divalent cations and some detergents |CITS:[terpstra80]|, the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; recombinant protein produced in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; was functional and required no lipids or detergents for activity |CITS:[11950974]|. Additionally, it was shown that the protein was equally efficient at pH 6.0 and 7.5; and stable at 4 ºC but less stable at 37 ºC. Its activity was greatly reduced in the presence of 0.2% to 1.0% (v/v) Triton X-100 or in acetone above 40% (v/v) |CITS:[11950974]|. Unlike its homologue |FRAME:AT5G43860|, expression of &lt;i&gt;CLH1&lt;/i&gt; is upregulated by jasmonate |CITS:[10611389]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G19670-MONOMER" />
